course: dictionary
lesson: 9723
title: A beam of light, and a ray of sunshine
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sunbeam is a narrow ray of sunlight. Look at the parts of the word: sun and beam. It is a beam of sunshine that you can see, often shining through a window, a gap or between clouds.
explain: The words around it give clues. «Dust dancing in a sunbeam» tells you the beam is bright enough to show tiny specks floating in the air. You can see the beam because the dust catches the light.
explain: Words can also describe people. Informally, in the UK, a cheerful child may be called «a sunbeam» or «a ray of sunshine». Nobody is really made of light, so the context tells you it means a bright, happy person.
fact: A sunbeam is a ray or narrow beam of sunlight.
fact: You can see a sunbeam because dust or water droplets scatter the light.
fact: Light travels in straight lines, which is why a sunbeam through a gap looks like a straight line.
fact: Informal, UK: «ray of sunshine» or «sunbeam» can be a fond name for a cheerful child.
fact: In stories, sunbeams make a scene feel warm and hopeful.
exercise: «The sunbeam came through the window and lit up the dusty room.» What is a sunbeam? | A window that is left open | A warm breeze from the sea | A dark cloud that hides the sun | *A narrow ray of sunlight
exercise: «Dust was dancing in the sunbeam.» Why can we see the sunbeam? | Sunbeams are painted on the glass | The beam makes a loud sound | The sun is very close to the ground | *Dust and water droplets scatter the light
exercise: A grandparent says, «You are our little ray of sunshine.» What does it probably mean? | You are very tall for your age | You are made of light | *You are a cheerful, happy child | You are lost in the sun
test: «Dust dancing in a sunbeam» shows the dust is doing what? | Turning into light | Falling to the floor in a heap | *Floating and moving in the light | Sleeping on the window sill
test: Why does a sunbeam through a gap look like a straight line? | *Light travels in straight lines | Dust makes the light go sideways | The sun is shaped like a line | Light bends around every corner
exam: Which sentence uses sunbeam to mean a cheerful child? | *She is such a sunbeam, always smiling at everyone | The sunbeam fell across the rug in the afternoon | A sunbeam came through the gap in the curtains | The sunbeam was hidden behind the clouds
